-- old 52-52 / new 52-52
OLD:       <p class="why-it-matters">A sequence of functions can converge at every point while its graphs stay far apart. The distinction matters for continuity: uniform convergence of continuous functions in the sense of Definition 1.2 guarantees that their limit is continuous (Proposition 2.1), and pointwise convergence in the sense of Definition 1.1 does not (Example 2.3). This lesson defines uniform convergence, proves that a uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous, shows that the pointwise version fails, and proves that the bounded functions, and the bounded continuous functions, are complete under the sup distance of L04, Example 1.6.</p>
NEW:       <p class="why-it-matters">A sequence of functions can converge at every point while its graphs stay far apart. The distinction matters for continuity: uniform convergence of continuous functions in the sense of Definition 1.2 guarantees that their limit is continuous (Proposition 2.1), and pointwise convergence in the sense of Definition 1.1 does not guarantee it (Example 2.3). This lesson defines uniform convergence, proves that a uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous, shows that the pointwise version fails, and proves that the bounded functions, and the bounded continuous functions, are complete under the sup distance of L04, Example 1.6.</p>
