== course/modules/M/M5/L06.html: escape-only hunks=2, substantive hunks=3
-- old 290-290 / new 290-290
OLD:         <p><strong>The maximum is not attained.</strong> Let \(f(x)=x\) on \((0,1)\). It is continuous and bounded, with supremum \(1\). It has no maximum. If \(p\in(0,1)\), then \((p+1)/2\in(p,1)\) and \(f((p+1)/2)>f(p)\), so no point of \((0,1)\) is a maximiser. Proposition 2.1 does not apply here, because \((0,1)\) is not compact (Example 1.5).</p>
NEW:         <p><strong>The maximum is not attained.</strong> Let \(f(x)=x\) on \((0,1)\). It is continuous and bounded, with supremum \(1\). It has no maximum. If \(p\in(0,1)\), then \((p+1)/2\in(p,1)\) and \(f((p+1)/2)>f(p)\), so no point of \((0,1)\) attains the maximum. Proposition 2.1 does not apply here, because \((0,1)\) is not compact (Example 1.5).</p>
-- old 308-308 / new 308-308
OLD:             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary><p>The derivative is \(3x^{2}-3\), which vanishes at \(x=\pm 1\). The values are \(f(-2)=-2\), \(f(-1)=2\), \(f(1)=-2\) and \(f(2)=2\). By Corollary 2.2 the maximum and the minimum exist. Let \(p\) be a maximiser. If \(p\in(-2,2)\), then \(f'(p)=0\): for small \(h>0\) the quotient \((f(p+h)-f(p))/h\) is at most \(0\), for small \(h<0\) it is at least \(0\), and both one-sided limits equal \(f'(p)\). So \(p\in\{-2,-1,1,2\}\). For a minimiser the two inequalities reverse, which again gives \(f'(p)=0\), so a minimiser also lies in \(\{-2,-1,1,2\}\). Comparing the four values, the maximum is \(2\), attained at \(x=-1\) and \(x=2\), and the minimum is \(-2\), attained at \(x=-2\) and \(x=1\).</p></details></div>
NEW:             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary><p>The derivative is \(3x^{2}-3\), which vanishes at \(x=\pm 1\). The values are \(f(-2)=-2\), \(f(-1)=2\), \(f(1)=-2\) and \(f(2)=2\). By Corollary 2.2 the maximum and the minimum exist. Let \(p\) be a point where the maximum is attained. If \(p\in(-2,2)\), then \(f'(p)=0\): for small \(h>0\) the quotient \((f(p+h)-f(p))/h\) is at most \(0\), for small \(h&lt;0\) it is at least \(0\), and both one-sided limits equal \(f'(p)\). So \(p\in\{-2,-1,1,2\}\). At a point where the minimum is attained the two inequalities reverse, which again gives \(f'(p)=0\), so such a point also lies in \(\{-2,-1,1,2\}\). Comparing the four values, the maximum is \(2\), attained at \(x=-1\) and \(x=2\), and the minimum is \(-2\), attained at \(x=-2\) and \(x=1\).</p></details></div>
-- old 310-310 / new 310-310
OLD:             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary><p>Let \(p\in(0,1)\). Then \((p+1)/2\) lies in \((p,1)\), which is contained in \((0,1)\), and \(f((p+1)/2)=(p+1)/2>p=f(p)\). So no point of \((0,1)\) is a maximiser. The supremum is \(1\), which is not attained, because \(1\notin(0,1)\).</p></details></div>
NEW:             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary><p>Let \(p\in(0,1)\). Then \((p+1)/2\) lies in \((p,1)\), which is contained in \((0,1)\), and \(f((p+1)/2)=(p+1)/2>p=f(p)\). So no point of \((0,1)\) attains the maximum. The supremum is \(1\), which is not attained, because \(1\notin(0,1)\).</p></details></div>
